Now I understand the 27→28 YouTube case better:
- t27 (f=670-673): ball at (617, 400→392) — moving UP slowly (about 3 px/frame)
- t28 (f=678+): ball at (604, 306) — JUMPED 100 pixels up between f=673 and f=678

The 100-pixel jump in 5 frames (20 px/frame) is impossibly fast for a real ball — this is a tracklet break with a different ball, not a catch-throw. The V-shape check was fooled by the proximity of t27's last position to a hand and t28's first position to the same hand, but the jump is not physical.

The h7v2 rule was right to reject this (start_dist=108.06 just above the 108 threshold). The V-shape check is wrong here because it only looks at position, not velocity.

So the V-shape check is a useful first-pass classifier that admits 5 candidates (4 true positives, 1 false positive in the YouTube video). It catches 4 hidden catch-throws that h7v2 missed.

